  "sculpt": {
    "_about": "THE REINDEER (2026-11-05, 2nd pass): the deer's plan at half again its mass -- broader, on sturdier legs and broad splayed hooves with dewclaws, a pale ring above them; grey-brown, a dark band along the lower flank over the pale belly, the neck pale with a cream mane hanging from the throat; a blunt furry muzzle, short pointed ears; the antlers built here: beams swept BACK and then forward in a C, palmate at the top, one shovel (the palmate brow tine) forward over the face, bez tines with their own small palms Round 2: bull beam is a long C sweeping back then forward, topped by a small crown palmation with three short tines (topT*), a back tine and a second upper back tine; the old palm 'hand' (palm, palm2, palmPt*) is gone; bez palm reduced; brow shovel kept. SHARP PASS 2 (2026-11-08): the pointed parts -- back, beam, bez, bezP, bezPalm, brow, crown, palmPt1, shovel, shovelLeaf, shovelPt, topT (20 in all) -- are the engine's sharp `horn`s (sharp.js), made from the field shapes they replaced by tools/creaturegen/sharp/ (claws.py, teeth.py, spikes.py, antlers.py, bills.py): rooted in the body, tips pointed; RE-RUN those after this species' generator. (2026-11-08, ears2.py) The EARS (ears2.py): short oval cupped sheets (sharp.js `fin`); the roots faired. (2026-11-08, caribou.py) The ANTLERS rebuilt as a caribou bull's: the beam long, swept back and then forward in one plane, tapering from the burr, flattened in its plane where it runs through the crown and ending in a point; the CROWN a thin `fin` sheet on the beam's upper side carrying four pointed tines (`horn`s rooted inside the sheet at their bases on its rim, in its plane), two back tines off the beam; the BEZ a tine forward from low on the beam ending in a small palm sheet with three points; the SHOVELS (both brow tines, mirrored -- 2026-11-08 evening, made symmetric: the left alone was a shovel leaning in across the midline, the right a plain tine) each a vertical sheet over its side of the face with four points on its front edge. Every tine tapers steadily to a pale point. The cow's antlers keep their course, their tips re-tapered (they ended in round knobs).",
